# outline_collection_group

Manages one explicit collection/group grant and its collection permission. It does not create or delete either parent, change group names or members, edit default collection access, or manage direct user grants or document grants. Destroy removes only the configured collection/group pair. Use the [collection user resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/resources/collection_user) for a separate direct user grant. Other grants and default workspace access can still give users access to the collection.

Use an unrestricted API key owned by an active workspace admin. The provider verifies the caller for every operation, including refresh and import. This resource targets Outline 1.10.1 only.

Externally linked and synchronized groups are supported. The collection-grant handlers authorize group read access, not group updates, and do not edit names or members. The restrictions on the [group resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/resources/group) and [group member resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/resources/group_member) do not apply here.

Archived collections cannot be managed or imported. Restore the collection outside Terraform first. Inaccessible collections return an error and retain state. A collection with the server's `admin` default permission is supported by this grant resource because it does not edit that default. The [collection resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/resources/collection) has a separate restriction on managing an `admin` default.

## Example usage

{{ tffile "examples/resources/outline_collection_group/resource.tf" }}

Reference the parents' `id` attributes when Terraform manages them, as above, so Terraform orders creation and destruction correctly. For existing parents, use their UUIDs or the [collection data source](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/data-sources/collection) and [group data source](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/data-sources/group). Use the group data source for synchronized groups rather than trying to manage their names or members.

`collection_id` and `group_id` are required canonical lowercase, nonzero UUIDs. Changing either requires replacement. The computed Terraform `id` is `collection_id/group_id`, with exactly one slash. It is not Outline's returned `GroupMembership.id` UUID.

`permission` is required and accepts only `read`, `read_write`, or `admin`. There is no default. Set the intended collection permission explicitly, including after import. This is not a group membership permission or a workspace role. Reads report the stored grant, not a summary of a user's effective access through all defaults and grants.

## Creation and permission changes

Create checks both parents and every page of the collection's group grants before writing. If the pair already exists, creation fails and requires import. Create and permission changes call `collections.add_group` with explicit intended permission. The resource never replaces the collection's entire group-grant set.

Outline 1.10.1 implements `collections.add_group` as an upsert. Another writer can add the same pair after preflight and before the write, and the provider can overwrite that writer's permission. There is no atomic create-only API to prevent this race. Coordinate writers for each pair, including other Terraform states and manual changes. Do not manage the same pair with multiple resources.

Reads and preflight use the complete, unfiltered, paginated `collections.group_memberships` response, specifically `data.groupMemberships`. The provider validates every page, pagination totals, duplicate grants, parent IDs, and permissions before using a result or declaring the pair absent. It completes the walk even after finding the target. A malformed or incomplete response is an error, not evidence of absence.

Refresh removes the resource from state only when a complete validated grant list proves the pair absent, or the existing release-verified parent reads prove a parent absent. Missing groups require the group helper's admin verification and complete workspace group list. Missing collections require the verified Outline `404 not_found` response from `collections.info`. A collection `403` never proves absence, even if a workspace list omits the UUID. Grant-endpoint `403` or `404` responses, failed parent checks, and malformed responses retain state and return an error.

## Failed-create recovery

After successful preflight, the provider retains the trusted `collection_id/group_id` in state before attempting the write. A timeout, error response, or failed response validation can follow a committed grant. The retained ID is a recovery handle, not proof that creation succeeded. Failed responses preserve state, and Terraform taints failed creates.

Inspect the actual grant and fix the reported error before retrying. If the pair exists and state still contains it, untaint the resource to reconcile it in place, then review the plan:

```shell
terraform untaint outline_collection_group.engineering
terraform plan
```

Use your resource's address. Do not accept replacement blindly, since it removes the grant before adding it again and can interrupt access. If state was lost, import the existing pair instead. If the pair is absent, a successful refresh removes the retained state so Terraform can create it again.

## Destroy behavior

Destroy calls `collections.remove_group` for only the configured pair. It does not delete the group, collection, or content. Removing the grant does not revoke access supplied by default workspace permission, direct user grants, or another group.

The server can refuse removing or downgrading the last collection-admin grant across direct user and group grants. Workspace-admin metadata access does not replace that stored grant. Server policy failures retain state; arrange another admin grant before retrying.

If removal races with another writer, the provider accepts the release's exact `400 invalid_request` response with message `This Group is not a part of the collection` only after a complete validated grant list confirms absence. Other `400` responses, `403` responses, and failed or incomplete confirmation reads return an error and retain state. The provider does not retry writes automatically.

To stop managing without removing the grant, back up state, remove its state entry with `terraform state rm outline_collection_group.engineering`, and remove its configuration before the next apply. Protect the backup as sensitive data. Keeping the resource block after removing state plans creation again, which refuses an existing pair.

